Detection rules for macOS from Elastic's rule set, https://github.com/elastic/detection-rules,
at commit 032ac5ecb0b4035a12b03dd25164e2fbcfae9fad, translated into Sigma.

Of the 185 rules there that apply to macOS, 66 are here as Sigma files, at the path their
upstream rule has under that repository's `rules/` folder (`macos/` or `cross-platform/`),
with `.yml` for `.toml`. Each file is a mechanical translation of the upstream rule it names,
and is not the upstream file. Its `references` link to the upstream file at that commit, and
its `elastic` block gives the upstream rule's `rule_id`, severity and risk score, the sha256 of
its query, the kinds of event it is evaluated over, what the translation left out of it, and
the fields it negates that may be absent. None is edited by hand. The translator is not part
of this repository. `SHA256SUMS` holds the sha256 of every file as the translator wrote it,
and a test fails when a file differs from it.

Each translation was checked against a reference that follows Elasticsearch's documented
meaning of the upstream query, on 33,969 invented events evaluated by `sigma-rust` 0.7.0, the
evaluator this program uses, over events built as this program builds them. They disagreed on
none.

The 15 rules whose `null_sensitive` list is not empty negate a field that an event may lack:
`process.args`, `process.parent.executable` or `user.id`. Their translation rests on an
assumption about how Elasticsearch treats a missing field: that a comparison with a field the
event does not have is false, so its negation holds.

`not-taken.tsv` lists the other 119 rules, each with its upstream path, rule id, name,
severity, the reason it was not taken and the translation's detail: `sequence` (it correlates
several events), `not_eql` (it is written in KQL or ES|QL), `needs_events` (it is about events
this program does not record), `needs_fields` (a part it cannot do without reads a field the
events do not give), `backslash_in_wildcard` (a pattern holds a backslash among wildcards,
whose meaning in Elasticsearch is not documented) and `spans_kinds` (it is about program
starts and file events at once).

The files are compiled into the program and evaluated by it. Nothing fetches rules at run
time.

The upstream rules are licensed under the Elastic License 2.0, as each file's `license` field
says ("Elastic License v2"). Its text is at https://www.elastic.co/licensing/elastic-license
and in the repository's `LICENSE.txt` at that commit,
https://github.com/elastic/detection-rules/blob/032ac5ecb0b4035a12b03dd25164e2fbcfae9fad/LICENSE.txt.
Each file names its authors in its `author` field, as the upstream rule does, and a message the
program makes from a match with a rule carries that rule's authors.
